Practical Case Study: Network Access Layer Overhaul at Pubali Bank PLC
Executive Summary & Background
Pubali Bank PLC, headquartered in Motijheel, Dhaka, Bangladesh, is executing an end-to-end modernization of its campus, data center, and branch network access infrastructure. To safeguard high-volume transactions, real-time core banking operations, and mobile banking APIs across hundreds of branches, the bank's IT & Infrastructure team—led by Senior Analyst Alex—has designed and implemented a unified Layer 2 Network Access & Wireless Framework.

This case study demonstrates the practical application of CCNA Domain 2.0 principles (VLANs, 802.1Q trunking, neighbor discovery, EtherChannel link aggregation, Rapid PVST+ loop prevention with protection features, split-MAC wireless architecture, and centralized WLC management) within a financial enterprise environment.


Section 1: Logical Segmentation & Trunking Infrastructure
To prevent broadcast storms from impacting core banking servers and enforce strict isolation between operational units, Pubali Bank PLC replaced its legacy flat network with a multi-VLAN structure.--------------------------------------------------------------------+
 (noneg)    ---------------------------------------------------------------------+
VLAN Architecture

•	VLAN 10 (Operations & Teller Workstations): Dedicated data VLAN for corporate endpoints.
•	VLAN 20 (IP Telephony): Voice VLAN configured on access ports using switchport voice vlan 20 to guarantee high priority and isolation for VoIP handsets.
•	VLAN 30 (Guest & Corporate Wireless): Assigned to wireless user segments.
•	VLAN 99 (Native VLAN): Configured specifically for untagged trunk traffic. VLAN 1 was explicitly removed from operational use, and all unused ports were administrative shut down and assigned to an isolated blackhole VLAN (VLAN 999). Reserved legacy VLANs (1002–1005) were left untouched as they cannot be deleted.

802.1Q Trunking & DTP Hardening
Inter-switch links between access and distribution switches run static IEEE 802.1Q trunking. Every frame crossing the trunk carries a 4-byte header insert containing:

•	TPID (0x8100): Identifies the frame as 802.1Q tagged.
•	PCP/CoS (3 bits): Priority markings for QoS routing.
•	VLAN ID (12 bits): Defines the destination VLAN (supporting VLAN IDs 1–4094).

To eliminate security vulnerabilities associated with Dynamic Trunking Protocol (DTP)—such as unauthorized trunk negotiation—DTP was disabled on all trunk links using switchport nonegotiate. Native VLANs were aligned to VLAN 99 on both ends of every trunk to prevent native VLAN mismatch alerts and cross-VLAN traffic leakage.


Section 2: Layer 2 Neighbor Discovery Protocols (CDP & LLDP)
For infrastructure visibility, network management, and VoIP power allocation, neighbor discovery protocols were deployed selectively across the bank's switching matrix.

Parameter / Feature	Cisco Discovery Protocol (CDP)	Link Layer Discovery Protocol (LLDP)
Standard	Cisco Proprietary	IEEE 802.1AB (Open Standard)
Default State	Enabled Globally (cdp run)	Disabled Globally (lldp run)
Transmit / Hold Timers	60 seconds / 180 seconds	30 seconds / 120 seconds
Destination MAC	01:00:0C:CC:CC:CC	01:80:C2:00:00:0E
Primary Use Case	Automatic discovery of Cisco IP Phones and Catalyst Switches	Discovery of multi-vendor APs, IP cameras, and non-Cisco appliances
Security Enforcement: Both CDP and LLDP disclose sensitive topology information (device IDs, software versions, and IP management addresses). Consequently, Alex issued no cdp enable and no lldp transmit on all edge access ports facing non-infrastructure host devices.

Section 3: High-Availability Link Aggregation (EtherChannel / LACP)
To eliminate bandwidth bottlenecks between distribution switches and the central data center without introducing switching loops, Pubali Bank PLC implemented multi-link EtherChannels governed by the open-standard Link Aggregation Control Protocol (LACP / IEEE 802.3ad).

