Section 5: Split-MAC Architecture & Enterprise Wireless (WLC)
For corporate Wi-Fi across Motijheel HQ and regional branch lobbies, Pubali Bank PLC deployed a Split-MAC Lightweight AP Architecture utilizing CAPWAP (Control and Provisioning of Wireless Access Points) tunnels.
+--------------------+
Lightweight AP Modes
1.	Local Mode (Default): The AP bridges wireless frames, executes real-time 802.11 functions, and tunnels all user data over CAPWAP to the central WLC.

2.	FlexConnect Mode: Deployed at remote branch offices across Bangladesh. If the WAN connection to the central WLC drops, the AP continues switching client traffic locally at the branch level without outage.

3.	Monitor / Rogue Detector Modes: Specialized non-client-serving modes dedicated to scanning RF space, capturing packets for security inspection, and identifying unauthorized rogue APs connected to the wired network.

Section 6: WLC Administration, AAA, and WLAN GUI Workflow
Administrative access and user authentication on the Cisco Wireless LAN Controller are governed by centralized AAA services.

Device Management vs. Network Access Authentication
•	Device Administration (TACACS+): Used exclusively for network engineers logging into the WLC GUI/CLI. Operates over TCP Port 49 and encrypts the entire packet payload.

•	User Authentication (RADIUS / 802.1X): Used for authenticating corporate Wi-Fi users. Operates over UDP Ports 1812 (Authentication) and 1813 (Accounting), encrypting only the user password field within the access request.

