# Domain 2.0 Network Access: Study Notes (20%)

## 1. VLANs

What: A VLAN is a separate Layer 2 broadcast domain inside the same physical switches. Normally 1 VLAN = 1 IP subnet.
- Analogy: departments sharing one office floor, with soundproof partitions. Finance can't hear Sales, even though they share the same building.

Data:
- Normal range: 1-1005. Extended range: 1006-4094.
- VLAN 1 is the default and can't be deleted. 1002-1005 are reserved legacy VLANs and can't be deleted.
- An access port belongs to one data VLAN, plus optionally one voice VLAN.
- Traffic between VLANs needs Layer 3: router-on-a-stick (subinterfaces) or an L3 switch (SVIs).

```
vlan 10
 name SALES
interface g0/1
 switchport mode access
 switchport access vlan 10
 switchport voice vlan 20
```

Verify: `show vlan brief`, `show interfaces g0/1 switchport`

Best practice: don't use VLAN 1. Shut unused ports and park them in an unused VLAN.

---

## 2. Trunking and 802.1Q

What: A trunk carries multiple VLANs over one link. 802.1Q inserts a 4-byte tag into the Ethernet frame.

Tag fields: TPID 0x8100 (16 bits), PCP/CoS (3 bits), DEI (1 bit), VLAN ID (12 bits).

Native VLAN: frames in this VLAN are sent *untagged*. The default is VLAN 1, and it must match on both ends.
- A mismatch causes a CDP native-VLAN mismatch log and VLAN leakage.

- Analogy: a shared corridor between two buildings where every parcel carries a colored department label (tag). Unlabeled parcels are assumed to belong to the "native" department, so both buildings must agree who that is.

```
interface g0/24
 switchport trunk encapsulation dot1q   ! only on platforms that also support ISL
 switchport mode trunk
 switchport trunk native vlan 99
 switchport trunk allowed vlan 10,20,99
 switchport nonegotiate
```

DTP results (Cisco-proprietary negotiation):

| Side A / Side B | Result |
|---|---|
| trunk / auto, desirable, or trunk | Trunk |
| desirable / auto or desirable | Trunk |
| auto / auto | Access |
| access / anything | Access |

Verify: `show interfaces trunk`, `show interfaces g0/24 switchport`

Trap: auto + auto never forms a trunk. Best practice is to set the mode manually and add `switchport nonegotiate`.

---

## 3. CDP and LLDP

What: Layer 2 neighbor discovery. They show only directly connected devices, because frames aren't forwarded.

-----------------------------------------------------------------
|                  | CDP               | LLDP                |
|------------------|-------------------|---------------------|
| Standard         | Cisco proprietary | IEEE 802.1AB (open) |
| Default on Cisco | Enabled           | Disabled |
| Timer / holdtime | 60 s / 180 s      | 30 s / 120 s |
| Dest. MAC        | 01:00:0C:CC:CC:CC | 01:80:C2:00:00:0E |
| Enable globally  | `cdp run`         | `lldp run` |
| Per interface    | `no cdp enable`   | `lldp transmit` / `lldp receive` |
| Verify           | `show cdp neighbors [detail]` | `show lldp neighbors [detail]` |
-------------------------------------------------------------------------------------

Shows: device ID, local and remote port, platform, capabilities, and management IP in `detail`.

- Analogy: swapping business cards with the person next to you. CDP is a card only Cisco people understand. LLDP is a universal card.

Security: disable both on untrusted or edge ports, since they leak device and IP information.

---

## 4. EtherChannel (LACP)

What: Bundles physical links into one logical Port-channel. STP sees one link, so no links are blocked.

Facts:
- Up to 8 active links per bundle (LACP also allows 8 standby).
- Load balancing is per flow by hash (MAC, IP, ports). One flow never exceeds one member's speed.
- The remaining links keep carrying traffic if one fails.

- Analogy: several lanes merged into one wide road. Each car stays in one lane (flow), and the road keeps running if one lane closes.

Protocols and modes:

---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
| Protocol | Standard               | Modes           | Forms when                    |
|----------|------------------------|-----------------|-------------------------------|
| LACP     | IEEE 802.3ad / 802.1AX | active, passive | active+active, active+passive |
| PAgP     | Cisco | desirable, auto | desirable+desirable, desirable+auto            |
| Static   | None  | on              | on+on only                                     |
---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------

Trap: passive + passive and auto + auto never form a channel. Never mix `on` with a protocol mode.

Rule: member ports must match in speed, duplex, switchport mode, native VLAN and allowed VLANs.

```
interface range g0/1-2
 channel-group 1 mode active
interface port-channel 1
 switchport mode trunk
```

Verify: `show etherchannel summary`. Flags: SU = Layer 2 and in use, P = bundled, D = down, I = stand-alone, s = suspended.

